-- title:
--   Proof of Theorem 4, p.4 — change of measure: $\mathbb E_{q_0}N\,\mathrm{kl}(q_0,u)\ge\mathrm{kl}(1-\delta,\delta)$
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be any audit rule (adaptive, with aborts and restarts, using old data and private randomness with a law $\nu$ that does not depend on the target parameter), at depth $d$ with survival $a\in(0,1]$, and let $0<\delta<\tfrac12$. Let $x,y\in(0,1)$ be two target parameters such that
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb P_x(R\text{ accepts})\ge1-\delta,\qquad \mathbb P_y(R\text{ accepts})\le\delta .
--   $$
--
--   Then, with $\mathrm{kl}(x,y)=x\log\frac xy+(1-x)\log\frac{1-x}{1-y}$,
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb E_xN\,\mathrm{kl}(x,y)\ \ge\ \mathrm{kl}(1-\delta,\delta).
--   $$
--
--   In the paper $x=q_0$ (a good parameter, where completeness gives the first inequality) and $y=u>t$ (a harmful parameter, where soundness gives the second). Only target observations carry information about the parameter; old data, navigation outcomes and the rule's decisions have common laws. This is the lower-bound half of Theorem 4 before the navigation multiplier is applied.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Expectations and the product are in $[0,\infty]$, so $\mathbb E_xN=\infty$ is allowed; kl is the published binary relative entropy `klBer`.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: x = q₀ and a harmful u ∈ (t, 1), with acceptance probability ≥ 1 − δ at q₀ (completeness) and ≤ δ at u (soundness) → any x, y ∈ (0, 1) with P_x(accept) ≥ 1 − δ and P_y(accept) ≤ δ; reason: the source's argument uses nothing else about q₀ and u; taking x = q₀, y = u gives the source's display
--   - encoding: any harmful u > t, including u = 1 → y < 1; reason: at u = 1 the source's kl(q₀, 1) is +∞ (division by zero inside the logarithm, outside the real formula's domain, where Lean's Real.log returns a junk value), so the only excluded case is the undefined one; the proof uses u ↓ t
--   - encoding: navigation and target outcomes with their stated conditional laws given the history; old transcript generated by a common kernel under all compared parameters; private randomness → side variable w ∼ ν (old data and private randomness, law free of q), independent of an i.i.d. Bernoulli(a) navigation tape and an i.i.d. Bernoulli(q) target tape; the k-th navigation attempt reads navigation entry k, the j-th target attempt reads target entry j (lawQ); reason: attempts are predictable, so each reads a fresh entry independent of everything observed; the joint law of (old data, randomness, fresh history) is the source's
--   - encoding: C the new physical cost (root setups, navigation attempts, target attempts), N the number of target observations, expectations E_q → cost and targetCount valued in ℕ∞, expectations as lintegrals in ℝ≥0∞ (expCost, expTargets); reason: same quantities; a rule that never stops has cost ⊤ and never accepts
--   - encoding: an auditor may adaptively abort or restart; root resets allowed, internal resets not → actions setup / attempt / accept / stop; setup issued during an episode abandons it; attempt with no active episode acts as a root setup (cost 1); decisions are measurable in w for each fresh history; reason: every abort/restart is a root setup; no action yields a free observation; measurability makes the acceptance events and costs well defined
-- source:
--   Q. Li, When Can Physical Evidence Survive a World-Model Update? Finite-cost re-auditing and reusable policy certificates, review revision of 3 November 2026 (STOC 2027 submission, sha256 a20c437b34115b84982339e9a6fa319cb3a66f66d9d4c474465cbb954c79093d), proof of Theorem 4, p.4 (PDF p.5), display "Eq0N kl(q0, u) ≥ kl(1 − δ, δ)"

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LimitedPriceChanges_LowerBound_KL
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_mu
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_Delta
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_Rule
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_run
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_lawQ

open MeasureTheory
open LimitedPriceChanges.LowerBound (klBer)

namespace PhysReaudit

/-- **Change of measure for an arbitrary audit** (Q. Li, *When Can Physical Evidence Survive a World-Model Update?*, review revision of 3 November 2026 (sha256 a20c437b…), proof of Theorem 4, p.4 (PDF p.5), display
"Eq0N kl(q0, u) ≥ kl(1 − δ, δ)").

For every side law `ν`, every rule `R` (adaptive, with aborts and restarts, using old data and private
randomness), every `0 < δ < 1/2` and every pair of target parameters `x, y ∈ (0, 1)`: if `R` accepts with
probability at least `1 − δ` under `x` and at most `δ` under `y`, then
`E_x N · kl(x, y) ≥ kl(1 − δ, δ)`.

**Formalization Note** The paper applies it with `x = q₀` and a harmful `y = u > t`; here `x, y` are any
interior parameters. Computed in `ℝ≥0∞`, so `E_x N = ⊤` is allowed. -/
theorem change_of_measure {W : Type} [MeasurableSpace W] (ν : Measure W) [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]
    (R : Rule W) {a : ℝ} {d : ℕ} (ha : 0 < a) (ha1 : a ≤ 1) {δ x y : ℝ} (hδ : 0 < δ)
    (hδ1 : δ < 1 / 2) (hx : x ∈ Set.Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1) (hy : y ∈ Set.Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1)
    (hgood : ENNReal.ofReal (1 - δ) ≤ lawQ ν a x {ω | accepts R d ω})
    (hbad : lawQ ν a y {ω | accepts R d ω} ≤ ENNReal.ofReal δ) :
    ENNReal.ofReal (klBer (1 - δ) δ) ≤ expTargets R ν a d x * ENNReal.ofReal (klBer x y) := by sorry

end PhysReaudit
